MISSION 1: Build the AI and ML Landscape
1. What is Artificial Intelligence (AI)?
Artificial Intelligence (AI) is a branch of computer science that develops machines and computer systems capable of performing tasks that normally require human intelligence.
These tasks include:
•	Learning from information. 
•	Solving problems. 
•	Understanding human language. 
•	Recognizing images and objects. 
•	Making decisions. 
•	Predicting future results. 
Example: Google Assistant understands our voice and responds to our questions using AI technologies.
2. Difference between AI, Rule-Based Programming and Machine Learning
Feature	Rule-Based Programming	Artificial Intelligence	Machine Learning
Meaning	Works using predefined rules.	Makes machines perform intelligent tasks.	Learns patterns from data.
Working	Follows IF-ELSE conditions.	Uses different intelligent techniques.	Uses algorithms and training data.
Learning	Cannot learn automatically.	May or may not learn.	Learns from data.
Changes	Programmer must change the rules.	Depends on the AI technique.	Can improve through training.
Example	Calculator	Expert system	Spam email detection
Explanation:
•	Rule-based programming: The programmer writes all the instructions. The computer follows those instructions. 
•	Artificial Intelligence: Machines perform tasks such as reasoning, decision-making and problem-solving. 
•	Machine Learning: Computers identify patterns from data and use those patterns to make predictions. 

3. Relationship Between AI, ML, Deep Learning, Generative AI and LLMs
These concepts are related to each other, but they are not all at the same level.

1. Artificial Intelligence (AI)
AI is the broad field of creating intelligent machines that can perform tasks like humans.
Example: Voice assistants and intelligent robots.
2. Machine Learning (ML)
ML is a subset of AI. It allows computers to learn from data without programming every individual decision.
Example: Email spam detection.
3. Deep Learning (DL)
Deep Learning is a subset of Machine Learning. It uses artificial neural networks with multiple layers to learn complex patterns.
Example: Face recognition.


4. Generative AI
Generative AI is a type of AI that creates new content based on patterns learned from existing data.
It can generate:
•	Text 
•	Images 
•	Audio 
•	Videos 
•	Computer code 
Example: ChatGPT generating answers to questions.
5. Large Language Models (LLMs)
LLMs are machine learning models based on deep learning that are trained on large amounts of text data to understand and generate human language.
Example: ChatGPT, Google Gemini and other language models.
4. Why is Automation Not Necessarily AI?
Automation means performing a task automatically without requiring continuous human involvement.
However, not all automation uses artificial intelligence.
Example:
A washing machine follows a fixed washing program. It washes clothes according to predefined instructions.
It does not learn from previous washing experiences or make intelligent decisions.
Therefore, it is automation but not necessarily AI.
Why AI Does Not Always Mean ML?
AI is a broad field. Some AI systems use predefined rules and logical reasoning instead of learning from data.
Example: A rule-based expert system makes decisions using predefined knowledge and conditions.
Therefore:
•	All Machine Learning is considered a part of AI. 
•	All AI systems do not necessarily use Machine Learning. 
•	Automation can work without AI. 
5. What is Explicitly Programmed vs What Can Be Learned from Data?
Explicitly Programmed	Learned from Data
Instructions are written by programmers.	Patterns are identified from training data.
Uses predefined rules.	Uses machine learning algorithms.
Does not automatically learn.	Can improve through training.
Programmer controls the conditions.	Model learns relationships between inputs and outputs.
Example: Calculator.	Example: Face recognition system.
Explanation:
In conventional programming, programmers provide instructions and conditions to the computer.
In Machine Learning, programmers provide data and a learning algorithm. The system learns patterns from the data and uses them to make predictions.
6. Five Important Distinctions in My Own Words
1.	AI vs ML: AI is the broad concept of making machines intelligent, whereas ML is a method that helps machines learn from data. 
2.	ML vs Deep Learning: ML uses different algorithms to identify patterns, whereas deep learning uses multiple neural network layers to learn complex patterns. 
3.	Deep Learning vs Generative AI: Deep learning is a learning technique, whereas generative AI uses learned patterns to create new content. 
4.	Generative AI vs LLM: Generative AI can create different types of content, whereas LLMs mainly focus on understanding and generating human language. 
5.	Automation vs AI: Automation follows instructions to complete tasks automatically, whereas AI can perform intelligent tasks such as reasoning, learning and decision-making. 
7. Three Examples Classified with Reasons
Example 1: Automatic Traffic Signal
•	Category: Rule-Based Automation. 
•	Reason: Traffic signals follow predefined timing and instructions to control traffic. 
•	They do not necessarily learn from traffic conditions. 
Example 2: Email Spam Detection
•	Category: Machine Learning. 
•	Reason: The system learns patterns from previously identified spam and normal emails. It uses those patterns to classify new emails. 
Example 3: ChatGPT
•	Category: Generative AI and Large Language Model. 
•	Reason: It uses a trained language model to understand user questions and generate human-like text responses. 
8. Conclusion
Artificial Intelligence is a broad technology that helps machines perform intelligent tasks. Machine Learning is a subset of AI that allows computers to learn from data. Deep Learning is a subset of ML that uses neural networks. Generative AI creates new content, and Large Language Models generate and understand human language.
Automation and AI are not the same because automation follows instructions, while AI can perform intelligent tasks. Understanding these differences helps us identify where each technology is used in real-world applications.

